ing bloating boosting your immune  system detoxing your body balancing  hormones slowing aging and of course  promoting weight loss just to name a few  now ingredients do vary from brand to  brand obviously but generally speaking  we're looking at a list of dehydrated  and powdered fruits and veggies extracts  vitamins minerals digestive enzymes  prebiotics probiotics herbs and other  plant-based ingredients in fact powdered  greens can contain up to 75 or more  different plant-based ingredients but  can we really solve all of our health  problems with a single jug of green  tinged water a day  um  well before we dig into the common  health claims i just want to note that  the evidence on powdered greens is  highly limited and most of the research  we do have is funded by the brand itself  or commercial seller of the product  which can create some bias in the  research there's also significant  variety in the specific ingredients and  amounts they contain making them  difficult to assess the category as a  whole so i'm just gonna work with the  evidence that we do have and look  specifically at some of the most common  ingredients that are used let's jump  into some of those claims number one it  improves our blood pressure so we  actually do have some research to  suggest that powdered greens may help  improve blood pressure for example one  small study found that daily intake of  greens powders specifically nano greens  helped to decrease blood pressure by  eight percent in folks with hypertension  but it's important to note that this  particular study was funded by biopharma  which is a company that sells nano green  supplements which of course like i said  may make the research subject to bias  however another study that was not  industry funded found similar blood  pressure lowering effects from daily  intake of greens powders in healthy  subjects while more research is needed  in this area we do know that higher  antioxidant intake may help to reduce  blood pressure markers and promote heart  health and considering powdered greens  are a concentrated source of  antioxidants it may be beneficial  however you can also totally get the  same effect just from eating a diet rich  in fruits and veg and that brings me to  claim number two greens powders boost  energy so powdered greens typically only  provide around 20 to 50 calories per  scoop now while you can certainly bump  up the calories by mixing it into a  smoothie or juice it is ultimately just  not an adequate replacement for actual  food so even though we are not getting  any significant energy boosting effects  from actual energy aka calories some  powdered greens are formulated with  green tea extract which of course  contains caffeine in fact one small  study found that women reported  increased energy levels after taking one  scoop of powdered greens with green tea  extract no  on the other hand other products may  have like an energy enhancing blend that  contains a variety of extracts powders  and amino acids however many of these  ingredients are either already produced  by the body naturally are readily  available from foods you're likely  eating or there is simply not enough  solid evidence to back up their use  ultimately you're better off getting  your energy from the most reliable og  source food next is clean number three  it boosts immunity  that smells like both many powdered  greens are often formulated with  vitamins like vitamin d zinc vitamin c  and selenium and they promise to boost  your immune system as a result but no  nutrient can boost your immune system  research suggests that in people who are  deficient in vitamin d supplementation  may help to lower the risk of  respiratory infections we also know that  vitamin c zinc and elderberry extract  may shorten the duration of a cold but  they're not likely so effective at  prevention some powdered greens will  also be formulated with an immune blend  with a variation of extracts like  astragalus 