ng to me is that while a lot of  influencers tout the anti-bloat benefits  i can see a lot of super bloating and  non-gut-friendly ingredients in this  list oh shocker mainly because they're  really high in fodmaps which can be  triggering for those with ibs fodmap  sensitivity or sibo for example so  ingredients like inulin fiber chicory  root extract garlic extract fructo  oligosaccharides apple powder asparagus  powder and shiitake mushroom powder are  all common sightings on these packages  and they're all common no-no's for a lot  of people dealing with persistent bloat  the other peculiar piece is the lack of  fiber greens powders are often seen as a  replacement for actual vegetables that  are good for your gut and sure if you're  fodmap sensitive i can see how consuming  less fiber from vegetables may  temporarily reduce bloating but as a  whole the best thing we can do to build  a healthy microbiome and to support gut  health is to get adequate fiber and most  powder greens products have only around  two grams now keep in mind most people  need between 25 and 38 grams of fiber  per day for regularity and other  benefits so i say add this to the list  of reasons why this is not an adequate  swap for actual food is it that obvious  i'm sorry with all that said while  powdered greens are unlikely to prevent  illness or drastically improve your  health they may still be a great add-on  to an already healthy balanced lifestyle  or may help to fill some nutritional  gaps and this is why i like to think of  them as a multivitamin in powdered form  and if you choose to opt for a green  space multi here are some things to look  for before you buy number one make sure  it's third party tested it's important  to look for whether the product is  third-party certified as this will  ensure that it has been tested for  quality and purity you can look up  supplements on websites like lab door  informed choice and nsf sport or check  the faq section of a brand's website or  if you still can't find info you can  also email the brand support team  directly and ask for proof of their  third party testing number two beware of  proprietary blends so i mentioned that  often brands will not disclose the  quantities of certain vitamins or  minerals and instead they just package  them under proprietary blends and i  understand the business reason for this  but as a consumer it means that you have  no idea how much of something is  actually in a product and whether it  yields a clinically effective dose  that's quite an accusation any response  seth  i always say that a piece of broccoli  could fart on a greens powder and they  could list it as an ingredient most of  the functional ingredients listed on  labels have clinical doses of between 1  to 10 grams each and since the scoop of  powder is generally in the 10 to 15 gram  range it's highly unlikely you're  getting a clinically relevant dose of  most of the ingredients you also could  hypothetically be getting a dangerously  high amount of an ingredient for you so  just like to keep in mind if you want to  know exactly what you're taking and  number three you want to avoid  sensationalized claims if the claim  sounds too good to be true it probably  is while supplements are not permitted  to be marketed with promises to treat  prevent or cure disease verbiage like  100  safe or zero side effects is also a bit  questionable in my books if a product  has the power to have a clinically  significant benefit it also comes with  risks and number four more is not  necessarily  more it is common for supplements to  provide nutrient quantities that are  significantly more than one's daily  needs and i know that we often see  things like 500 of your daily needs  intakes and think woo that's great more  is better but in some cases this may  actually do more harm than good for  example supplemental forms of vitamin c  are not excreted as easily from the body  compared to food sources and super high  amounts can also cause digestive  symptoms like diarrhea nausea and  abdominal cramps that's horrifying on  the other hand if you're already  obtaining enough of said vitamins from  food sources a supplemental high dose  will do nothing but create expensive pee  so when i see a lot of wellness  influencers using greens powders every  day and then following that up with like  a green smoothie and an avocado toast  and like a massive salad i'm like  you probably are not the kind of person  who might actually benefit from  something like this so again make sure  that you're reading ingredient labels  carefully especially if you are taking  other supplements on the side or getting  enough of these nutrients from food so  in conclusion while i think that  powdered greens can be a great way to  fill in nutritional gaps and get a  concentrated dose of antioxidants to  support overall health and well-being  they should be seen as multi-vitamins  not as a replacement for fruits and  vegetables in other words powdered  greens shouldn't be seen as the end-all  be-all to health and we should still be  prioritizing healthy lifestyle behaviors  like eating a balanced diet regular  exercise quality sleep and stress  management i know it's not as easy and  sexy and fast but if you enjoy the taste  of powdered greens you don't mind the  expense and they don't cause any  digestive discomfort or other side  effects then they can be totally used  within the context of a healthy  lifestyle and if not there are a lot of  other more budget-friendly multivitamins  or even just food that can provide the  same benefits for your health so on that  note that is all that i have for you  guys today if you like this video be  sure to give it the thumbs up leave me a  comment below on who or what you'd like  senior view next subscribe to the  channel and i'll see you next time on  abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

